::: {.doc-subject}

Find a minimum-weight branching of a directed graph (Edmonds' algorithm).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L80378-L80469){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.minimum_branching(
    weight_attr=None,
    include=None,
    exclude=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Minimum branching](../assets/graphs/minimum-branching.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A weighted directed triangle: A→B (−2), A→C (−1), C→B (−3), and B→C (4). Only negative edges lower the total, and each node may keep one parent, so the minimum branching takes C→B and A→C, in the accent color, while A→B and B→C are faded. The card lists the two edges and their total weight −3 − 1 = −4." width="100%"}

A branching is a directed forest in which every node has at most one incoming edge.
The minimum branching has the smallest possible total weight. Because the empty
branching (no edges at all) has weight zero, only edges with *negative* weight can
lower the total, so on a graph whose weights are all positive the result has no edges.
That matches NetworkX, and makes this method useful mainly for signed costs (negative
values meaning gains). To connect every node as cheaply as possible, use
`minimum_spanning_arborescence()` instead.

`include=` forces edges into the result and `exclude=` rules edges out. Self-loops are
never chosen, and the original graph is not modified.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Name of the edge attribute holding the weights. If `None` (the default), each edge's
    weight field is used (`1.0` on an unweighted graph), as it is for `"weight"`. Under any
    other name, every edge must hold a number.

<code>[include]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Edges the result must contain, as edge IDs or `(u, v)` node pairs (a pair stands for
    every edge from `u` to `v`).

<code>[exclude]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Edges the result must not contain, given the same way.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A new directed graph with every node of the original (IDs, labels and attributes
    kept) and only the branching's edges (weights and attributes copied).
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is undirected, an edge value is NaN, `weight_attr=` names an attribute that
    an edge lacks or holds as a non-number, or the included edges don't form a branching (a
    node with two included in-edges, or an included cycle).
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The minimum branching is the maximum branching for the negated weights, found with
Edmonds' cycle-contraction algorithm in $O(V E)$ time; see `maximum_branching()`.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~maximum_branching`) 

:   The branching with the largest total weight.

[](`~minimum_spanning_arborescence`) 

:   The cheapest branching that reaches every node from a root.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

With signed costs, the minimum branching keeps the negative edges (the gains), as long
as no node gets two parents and no cycle forms. `B` takes its cheaper parent `C`, and
`C` keeps `A`, for a total of -4:

::: {#6b5a8119 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B", -2.0), ("C", "B", -3.0),
     ("A", "C", -1.0), ("B", "C", 4.0)],
    directed=True,
)
branching = graph.minimum_branching()
branching.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['C->B', 'A->C']
```
:::
:::


::: {#9043deb2 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.plot(
    highlight_edges=branching.get_edge_ids(), edge_label_by="weight",
    label_by="label", seed=1,
)
```

